WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.350 --> 00:00:07.899
CNBC Television: This is the most severe shortage
of semiconductors in years.

00:00:07.899 --> 00:00:12.670
KVUE ABC: The U.S. Senate passed a bill that
would give manufacturers more incentives to

00:00:12.670 --> 00:00:14.460
make semiconductor chips.

00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:18.420
CNA: Taiwan wants to make sure that it keeps
its leading position in the semiconductor

00:00:18.420 --> 00:00:21.760
industry, the sector has been caught up in
U.S.-China trade tensions.

00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:25.529
Joe BIDEN: We are in competition with China
and other countries to win the 21st Century.

00:00:25.529 --> 00:00:28.970
We are at a great inflection point in history.

00:00:28.970 --> 00:00:34.630
SIERRA: Right now you re hearing my voice
because of a silicon chip. Hundreds of them

00:00:34.630 --> 00:00:41.370
in fact. You may know them by other terms,
like microchip or semiconductor. As the world

00:00:41.370 --> 00:00:45.970
has digitized, chips have become the lifeblood
of innovation and the global economy, they

00:00:45.970 --> 00:00:48.890
re in almost everything we use.

00:00:48.890 --> 00:00:54.660
Access to chips can make or break a country
s economic future, and as shortages pile up,

00:00:54.660 --> 00:00:59.000
countries are scrambling to figure out how
to ensure their access.

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:04.369
At the heart of the story is one company,
TSMC, which manufactures chips that are found

00:01:04.369 --> 00:01:10.659
in everything from iPhones to fighter jets.
No other company can do what they do, and

00:01:10.659 --> 00:01:16.720
as a result, the world depends on them. But
TSMC is located on the island of Taiwan, and

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:21.090
that puts it at the center of dangerous tensions
between China and the United States.

00:01:21.090 --> 00:01:28.049
I m Gabrielle Sierra and this is Why It Matters,
today, our fragile relationship with the chips

00:01:28.049 --> 00:01:32.110
that power our world.

00:01:32.110 --> 00:01:38.000
SIERRA: So what is a silicon chip?

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:44.640
CLARK:Chips are the descendants of the original
transistor, which was a - kind of a switching

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:49.290
device. It can do all kinds of things. And
what happened is they started to put many,

00:01:49.290 --> 00:01:52.619
many transistors on an individual piece of
silicon.

00:01:52.619 --> 00:01:57.620
SIERRA: This is Don Clark. He has been writing
about tech for more than 30 years, including

00:01:57.620 --> 00:02:02.470
23 as a reporter for the Wall Street Journal.
These days he s a contributor for the New

00:02:02.470 --> 00:02:03.470
York Times.

00:02:03.470 --> 00:02:09.479
CLARK: Now, the latest silicon chips can have
billions of tiny, tiny transistors on there

00:02:09.479 --> 00:02:12.360
that can store data and do calculations.

00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:20.680
HEILWEIL: So, chips are essentially the little
electronic foundation to the entire global

00:02:20.680 --> 00:02:21.850
tech economy.

00:02:21.850 --> 00:02:26.300
SIERRA: This is Rebecca Heilweil. She is a
reporter at Vox Recode.

00:02:26.300 --> 00:02:30.620
HEILWEIL: These little chips are basically
powering the ins and outs of all the little

00:02:30.620 --> 00:02:33.860
decisions that make up the devices that we
use.

00:02:33.860 --> 00:02:40.090
CLARK: A chip is now used in just about everything
you can imagine. I was fighting this morning

00:02:40.090 --> 00:02:44.310
with my espresso machine because it's got
chips in it, it was telling me that I couldn't

00:02:44.310 --> 00:02:48.340
do it because I hadn't done the mandatory
cleaning it was monitoring for.

00:02:48.340 --> 00:02:53.280
HEILWEIL: Your refrigerator, your computer,
any video game console you have, you know,

00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:54.340
your phone.

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:59.739
CLARK: Your microwave, your dishwasher, your
Alexa smart speaker, your car, there are many

00:02:59.739 --> 00:03:01.090
chips in your car.

00:03:01.090 --> 00:03:06.590
HEILWEIL: Any kind of electronic device has
chips, and how advanced they are depends on

00:03:06.590 --> 00:03:10.830
what kind of device you're using but they
re basically in everything right now.

00:03:10.830 --> 00:03:16.129
SIERRA: And it's not just consumer products.
The construction equipment we use to build

00:03:16.129 --> 00:03:22.090
homes, schools and offices requires chips.
The medical field relies heavily on them too.

00:03:22.090 --> 00:03:26.400
And if that wasn t enough, there s another
huge driver for chip demand...data centers.

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:32.370
You know, the massive server systems that
power the entire internet, from Google to

00:03:32.370 --> 00:03:37.780
Linkedin to Reddit. Every time you use these
services, there are chips on the other side

00:03:37.780 --> 00:03:39.810
providing the information.

00:03:39.810 --> 00:03:45.379
CLARK: People particularly notice this now
that we're streaming everything in our lives,

00:03:45.379 --> 00:03:51.349
from Netflix, to homeschooling to whatever.
But for the big broadcasters in the old days,

00:03:51.349 --> 00:03:56.170
if they were broadcasting over the air, people
could log on with their TVs, but they didn't

00:03:56.170 --> 00:03:59.709
have to do anything to their facilities, they
were just beaming the signals to all these

00:03:59.709 --> 00:04:05.910
different people. In the broadband streaming
era, each person that logs on, basically,

00:04:05.910 --> 00:04:11.769
they have to buy more computing capacity to
serve that person. So the more streams there

00:04:11.769 --> 00:04:13.740
are, the more chips are consumed.

00:04:13.740 --> 00:04:19.100
SIERRA: Every photo uploaded to Instagram,
every video on YouTube. All of it requires

00:04:19.100 --> 00:04:24.940
more computing power. To stay in the tech
race, countries need a steady stream of new

00:04:24.940 --> 00:04:29.611
chips. But before we can understand their
geopolitical importance, we first have to

00:04:29.611 --> 00:04:35.830
understand a bit more about how the chip industry
works. So, we all know that computing power

00:04:35.830 --> 00:04:40.580
gets better over time. Every few years we
re offered a new generation of smartphones,

00:04:40.580 --> 00:04:45.720
gaming consoles, and laptops that are faster
and more powerful than ever before. A lot

00:04:45.720 --> 00:04:51.740
of this progress is fueled by improvements
to chips. But it doesn t just happen. In order

00:04:51.740 --> 00:04:56.650
to push forward, scientists and engineers
have to figure out how to pack more and more

00:04:56.650 --> 00:05:02.639
transistors into the same space, and companies
invest billions to help them do it. When they

00:05:02.639 --> 00:05:10.500
succeed, the payoff is huge. Last year the
semiconductor industry reported $439 billion

00:05:10.500 --> 00:05:12.000
in revenue.

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:18.370
SIERRA: We must be making them by the millions
right now. I mean, how big is the demand?

00:05:18.370 --> 00:05:19.370
CLARK: Well, trillions.

00:05:19.370 --> 00:05:20.370
SIERRA: Trillions!

00:05:20.370 --> 00:05:24.380
CLARK: I mean, literally, trillions of chips
get made every year. And they go from the

00:05:24.380 --> 00:05:30.060
price of less than a penny to thousands of
dollars. So there's many different varieties

00:05:30.060 --> 00:05:31.060
of these things.

00:05:31.060 --> 00:05:38.740
SIERRA: Do you think that you can give me
a sort of step by step of how a chip is produced?

00:05:38.740 --> 00:05:40.770
What do the supply chains look like?

00:05:40.770 --> 00:05:46.580
CLARK: The supply chains are incredibly complicated.
It starts with raw materials. So you have

00:05:46.580 --> 00:05:52.400
silicon, which is the ingredient in sand.
And so they make this big cylinder thing.

00:05:52.400 --> 00:05:57.710
And then they slice it up and make wafers,
which are usually 12 inches across. And then

00:05:57.710 --> 00:06:03.960
they start laying down and taking off materials
from those wafers. And the most important

00:06:03.960 --> 00:06:09.599
step is called photolithography where they
trace the patterns of circuitry on those for

00:06:09.599 --> 00:06:15.620
each chip that's going to be made on the wafer.
There's a particular company called ASML in

00:06:15.620 --> 00:06:21.870
the Netherlands, which dominates that photolithography
step. But they now make a machine called an

00:06:21.870 --> 00:06:27.139
extreme ultraviolet lithography machine, each
one of these machines is about $150 million

00:06:27.139 --> 00:06:32.400
a pop. It's probably the most complicated
machine humans have ever created. And they

00:06:32.400 --> 00:06:38.410
are the only supplier of it. After that, all
these chemicals are added, things are etched,

00:06:38.410 --> 00:06:44.280
things are baked. Things are laid on. Anyway,
once they have that wafer created, they cut

00:06:44.280 --> 00:06:49.320
it up with these diamond saws to create the
individual chips, and they're sent to separate

00:06:49.320 --> 00:06:53.170
companies that wrap them in plastic and make
little connectors around them. And then they

00:06:53.170 --> 00:06:59.000
test them. And so really, the whole supply
chain gets really complicated because it includes

00:06:59.000 --> 00:07:04.500
all the raw materials. It includes all those
companies that make machines that are used

00:07:04.500 --> 00:07:10.050
to do all these processing steps. It includes
the companies that have the big factories,

00:07:10.050 --> 00:07:14.960
the big fabs, they're called like TSMC, and
Intel and Samsung. And then there's the companies

00:07:14.960 --> 00:07:21.259
that package them and test them. And then
various levels of purchasers and buyers and

00:07:21.259 --> 00:07:27.280
sellers. So they'd say now this, by some estimates,
that the average chip crosses a national border

00:07:27.280 --> 00:07:30.349
70 times before it reaches its end destination.

00:07:30.349 --> 00:07:36.199
SIERRA: Wow. That's quite a lot of steps.
Quite a lot of travel for one little chip.

00:07:36.199 --> 00:07:42.039
SIERRA: And guess what? If we thought demand
was high before, the pandemic kicked it into

00:07:42.039 --> 00:07:49.819
overdrive. Netflix added 37 million users.
Zoom went from 10 million to 300 million daily

00:07:49.819 --> 00:07:55.500
meeting participants. All of these processes
need chips. To understand this better, we

00:07:55.500 --> 00:08:01.449
spoke to Ajit Manocha, the president and CEO
of SEMI, the world s leading semiconductor

00:08:01.449 --> 00:08:02.699
industry association.

00:08:02.699 --> 00:08:09.090
MANOCHA: So we're on a growth path to start
with, and then the pandemic hit us. Now, what

00:08:09.090 --> 00:08:14.819
did the pandemic do? Immediately we went homeschooling,
working from home. We stopped coming to the

00:08:14.819 --> 00:08:21.110
offices. Social media companies became pretty
big. The number of laptops and the mobile

00:08:21.110 --> 00:08:26.970
devices that we needed last year was unprecedented.
I mean, I give the example, I have three grandkids

00:08:26.970 --> 00:08:31.240
in the ages of three to six. Each one of them
have two devices. They are homeschooling,

00:08:31.240 --> 00:08:36.330
they have their own laptop or iPad, and they
know how to turn it on and how to communicate

00:08:36.330 --> 00:08:41.740
with the class, how to sit in the Zoom classroom.
So, imagine it's going on all over the world.

00:08:41.740 --> 00:08:46.300
Virtually, every semiconductor company is
reporting record revenue. The demand is high.

00:08:46.300 --> 00:08:51.510
SIERRA: When supply chains are stretched to
their max, and demand continues to increase,

00:08:51.510 --> 00:08:57.010
you end up with shortages. And that s exactly
what happened over the last year. The consequences

00:08:57.010 --> 00:09:02.310
were so serious that even now, as vaccinations
are rolling out in many countries, shortages

00:09:02.310 --> 00:09:07.459
and product delays continue to pile up. And
the product that got hit hardest during the

00:09:07.459 --> 00:09:12.450
pandemic is not one you d immediately associate
with chips....cars.

00:09:12.450 --> 00:09:18.860
SIERRA: You've written about how over the
last year, we've seen this chip shortage due

00:09:18.860 --> 00:09:23.920
to the pandemic affecting global supply chains.
So do you think you can take me sort of step

00:09:23.920 --> 00:09:29.000
by step through that story, sort of how it
started and who it ended up affecting?

00:09:29.000 --> 00:09:34.300
HEILWEIL: Sure. So at the beginning of the
pandemic, some automakers were sort of estimating,

00:09:34.300 --> 00:09:38.810
okay, the pandemic means there's going to
be an economic crisis. And obviously, people

00:09:38.810 --> 00:09:43.339
aren't going to be buying as many cars. So
we're going to basically jump off the line

00:09:43.339 --> 00:09:49.370
for chips, because we don't think we're going
to need as many. Then suddenly, things reverse.

00:09:49.370 --> 00:09:54.829
And the economic recovery starts happening
again. And some car manufacturers want to

00:09:54.829 --> 00:09:59.270
basically get back on line, get their chips,
but suddenly, there's all these device makers

00:09:59.270 --> 00:10:05.329
who have been demanding chips the entire time.
And now, the automakers are still dealing

00:10:05.329 --> 00:10:11.290
with that shortage and trying to catch up.
And at the baseline, it takes a while to get

00:10:11.290 --> 00:10:15.880
a chip, it's not like you can just call up
a chip manufacturer and say, Hi, I need like

00:10:15.880 --> 00:10:20.269
10,000 more chips next week, can you just
make some extra, that's not how it works.

00:10:20.269 --> 00:10:26.460
We're talking about weeks and weeks and weeks
of lead time. And now we see GM and companies

00:10:26.460 --> 00:10:31.459
like that having to leave their workers idle
and, and close down factories, because there

00:10:31.459 --> 00:10:36.380
aren't enough chips to keep their manufacturing
going. And in the US, that's a real pain point,

00:10:36.380 --> 00:10:40.190
because there are so many auto workers and
that's a population a lot of people really,

00:10:40.190 --> 00:10:41.910
really care about.

00:10:41.910 --> 00:10:47.620
CBS46: Car dealerships are bracing for lean
months as computer chips shortages are causing

00:10:47.620 --> 00:10:50.480
supply nightmares thanks to the pandemic.

00:10:50.480 --> 00:10:53.210
CBS46: Kia will shut down for two days due
to supply issues.

00:10:53.210 --> 00:10:57.459
Reuters: Hyundai is the latest firm to say
it is paying a price for the global shortage

00:10:57.459 --> 00:10:58.459
of computer chips.

00:10:58.459 --> 00:11:02.610
ABC10: It s not just here we are feeling the
impact, it is everywhere throughout our nation.

00:11:02.610 --> 00:11:08.839
Now keep in mind the vast majority of new
cars are built with these computer chips.

00:11:08.839 --> 00:11:12.900
So in short, no computer chips, no new cars.

00:11:12.900 --> 00:11:17.100
SIERRA: That seems like a real fragile system
right there.

00:11:17.100 --> 00:11:22.519
CLARK: It is fragile. And, back when we were
having a shortage of personal protective equipment

00:11:22.519 --> 00:11:26.980
coming from China, people started thinking,
hey, you know, some things are strategic,

00:11:26.980 --> 00:11:32.140
we can't rely on foreign places to supply
some of these things that are just really

00:11:32.140 --> 00:11:37.100
necessary. So between the shutdown of the
auto plants, which cost a lot of union workers

00:11:37.100 --> 00:11:42.010
their jobs, I mean, that puts a lot of pressure
on a democratic president. Meanwhile, you

00:11:42.010 --> 00:11:46.850
had this kind of gradual feeling that the
US was getting too dependent on foreign suppliers,

00:11:46.850 --> 00:11:52.560
even allies in Taiwan and South Korea. So
there's been a separate push in Washington

00:11:52.560 --> 00:11:58.980
to help the semiconductor industry and they're
now proposing a $50 billion infusion to help

00:11:58.980 --> 00:12:00.890
spur more US manufacturing.

00:12:00.890 --> 00:12:07.889
SIERRA: In fact, since our interview with
Don, the Senate passed a $250 billion bill

00:12:07.889 --> 00:12:13.949
that aimed at boosting U.S. tech competitiveness,
including $52 billion for chip production.

00:12:13.949 --> 00:12:19.140
And the U.S. isn t alone. Countries all over
the world have recognized that access to chips

00:12:19.140 --> 00:12:23.870
is essential, and they are making big investments
in an attempt to reduce their dependence on

00:12:23.870 --> 00:12:30.730
other countries. The epicenter of that dependence
is TSMC, located one hundred miles off China

00:12:30.730 --> 00:12:37.040
s coast on the island of Taiwan. TSMC s rise
has left rivals like Intel almost a decade

00:12:37.040 --> 00:12:42.389
behind, and made it one of the world s most
valuable companies. At the same time it has

00:12:42.389 --> 00:12:47.260
become a target of U.S.-China competition,
and according to some analysts, could even

00:12:47.260 --> 00:12:52.930
pull the United States into armed conflict.
More on that after the break.

00:12:52.930 --> 00:13:02.040
SIERRA: I wanted to tell you about another
podcast I think you ll like: The Last Archive

00:13:02.040 --> 00:13:07.630
from Harvard historian and New Yorker writer
Jill Lepore. This season, Lepore is investigating

00:13:07.630 --> 00:13:14.940
purveyors of doubt hoaxers, fraudsters, pseudoscientists
and how the peddling of doubt evolved over

00:13:14.940 --> 00:13:21.750
the 20th century, leading all the way to the
Capitol Insurrection on January 6, 2021. This

00:13:21.750 --> 00:13:27.120
show is unlike anything you ve heard before:
history animated by long lost archival tapes

00:13:27.120 --> 00:13:33.329
and documents, intrepid reporting, and radio
drama reenactments. The Last Archive unfurls

00:13:33.329 --> 00:13:39.959
like a classic 1930s gumshoe mystery but takes
on the big issues of today. How do we know

00:13:39.959 --> 00:13:45.480
what we know, and why does it seem, sometimes,
lately, as if we don t know anything at all?

00:13:45.480 --> 00:13:52.170
Listen to The Last Archive wherever you get
your podcasts. From Pushkin Industries.

00:13:52.170 --> 00:13:59.460
SIERRA: To understand TSMC s enormous power,
you have to understand their place in the

00:13:59.460 --> 00:14:07.040
global semiconductor industry. Companies like
Intel, Samsung, and AMD design chips. TSMC,

00:14:07.040 --> 00:14:12.230
on the other hand, doesn t design chips at
all, it simply takes the designs from clients

00:14:12.230 --> 00:14:17.270
and then manufactures them. It wasn t always
this way. Most designers used to manufacture

00:14:17.270 --> 00:14:23.170
their own chips. Intel still does. But as
the semiconductor business grew over the years,

00:14:23.170 --> 00:14:28.269
skyrocketing demand and high manufacturing
costs prompted many designers to outsource

00:14:28.269 --> 00:14:32.170
the manufacturing process to third-party fabrication
plants.

00:14:32.170 --> 00:14:38.800
MANOCHA: Morris Chang who is founder of TSMC,
he came up with the idea that why not we create

00:14:38.800 --> 00:14:45.350
a wafer fab foundry, where we will actually
put all the investments and manufacture for

00:14:45.350 --> 00:14:49.810
the companies who are very good in designing
chips. And the manufacturing companies will

00:14:49.810 --> 00:14:55.009
take the blueprints and they will start fabricating
based upon what the chip is supposed to be

00:14:55.009 --> 00:15:01.321
in final shape. And TSMC did a very good job
of making that happen, and pretty soon, people

00:15:01.321 --> 00:15:05.949
realize this is very good business for us.
Now, we can spend more money and time and

00:15:05.949 --> 00:15:12.450
effort on chip design and design a lot more
chips instead of spending more money on building

00:15:12.450 --> 00:15:13.450
fabs.

00:15:13.450 --> 00:15:19.680
SIERRA: TSMC has gotten better at it than
anyone else on earth, earning 39,000 patents

00:15:19.680 --> 00:15:25.290
for their manufacturing process along the
way. Today they serve over 500 clients, including

00:15:25.290 --> 00:15:30.810
Apple, Sony, Huawei, Alibaba, and the U.S.
Department of Defense.

00:15:30.810 --> 00:15:35.389
SACKS: When you look at the company, it's
staggering. They just announced that they're

00:15:35.389 --> 00:15:41.389
going to invest $100 billion in the next three
years. They are absolutely at the leading

00:15:41.389 --> 00:15:44.420
edge and there are very few, if any competitors.

00:15:44.420 --> 00:15:50.399
SIERRA: $100 billion, by one company, that
s almost twice as much as the spending approved

00:15:50.399 --> 00:15:55.430
by the U.S. Senate. This is David Sacks, by
the way. He is a research fellow at the Council,

00:15:55.430 --> 00:16:01.579
where his work focuses on U.S.-China relations,
U.S.-Taiwan relations, and Chinese foreign

00:16:01.579 --> 00:16:02.579
policy.

00:16:02.579 --> 00:16:12.240
SACKS: So around 90% of the world's most advanced
chips are made by TSMC on Taiwan. And of course,

00:16:12.240 --> 00:16:18.130
Taiwan is a geopolitical flashpoint for the
United States and for China.

00:16:18.130 --> 00:16:24.571
SIERRA: Taiwan and China have a volatile relationship,
which puts global chip supplies at risk. In

00:16:24.571 --> 00:16:29.880
order to understand this risk, we need to
do a bit of history. In the aftermath of World

00:16:29.880 --> 00:16:35.990
War II, China underwent a long and bloody
civil war. There were two factions: the Nationalists,

00:16:35.990 --> 00:16:41.279
and the Chinese Communist Party. In 1949,
the Nationalists were defeated, and fled to

00:16:41.279 --> 00:16:47.110
their stronghold on the island of Taiwan.
Since then, Taiwan has been governed independently

00:16:47.110 --> 00:16:53.279
from mainland China, with its own currency,
passports, and military. Still, Beijing maintains

00:16:53.279 --> 00:16:58.640
that Taiwan is and always will be part of
its territory. For a long time now the schism

00:16:58.640 --> 00:17:03.970
has been a very touchy issue; for China, for
Taiwan, and for countries that want to interact

00:17:03.970 --> 00:17:09.870
with them. At the heart of the problem is
a single worry: will China someday invade

00:17:09.870 --> 00:17:13.939
Taiwan in an attempt to reunify the island?

00:17:13.939 --> 00:17:21.169
SACKS: Today Taiwan is a thriving democracy
with multiple peaceful transitions of power,

00:17:21.169 --> 00:17:26.919
firm civilian control over the military and
a vibrant civil society. And from the Chinese

00:17:26.919 --> 00:17:33.860
perspective, Taiwan is viewed as a symbol
that the civil war is not yet over. They believe

00:17:33.860 --> 00:17:41.330
that Taiwan has to become a part of China
sooner or later, and they have reserved the

00:17:41.330 --> 00:17:47.740
right to use force to bring that about. The
United States official position is that Taiwan's

00:17:47.740 --> 00:17:52.871
future is to be determined. So we don't take
a view on Taiwan's legal status. But what

00:17:52.871 --> 00:17:58.730
we do emphasize is that anything that happens
with respect to Taiwan has to come about peacefully

00:17:58.730 --> 00:18:05.100
and with the consent of the people on Taiwan.
But still the United States has certain commitments

00:18:05.100 --> 00:18:11.830
to Taiwan under the Taiwan Relations Act,
namely to provide Taiwan with defensive weapons

00:18:11.830 --> 00:18:18.580
and to maintain the capacity to come to Taiwan's
defense without actually saying that the United

00:18:18.580 --> 00:18:22.059
States will come to Taiwan's defense during
a crisis.

00:18:22.059 --> 00:18:28.170
SIERRA: If you re a bit confused it s not
your fault. U.S. policy on Taiwan is ambiguous

00:18:28.170 --> 00:18:34.550
by design, and it centers on having the ability
to defend Taiwan from China while not committing

00:18:34.550 --> 00:18:40.350
to do so. It has always been a very touchy
situation. And now the world s most advanced

00:18:40.350 --> 00:18:44.020
and important chip manufacturer has been added
to the mix.

00:18:44.020 --> 00:18:50.049
SACKS: If China is able to take over Taiwan,
it can project power into the Pacific from

00:18:50.049 --> 00:18:55.380
the east coast of Taiwan. It can essentially
regulate shipping in the South China sea and

00:18:55.380 --> 00:19:01.390
exert leverage on shipping lanes that end
up in South Korea and Japan. So those are

00:19:01.390 --> 00:19:06.409
the traditional arguments that have been given
for why the United States should defend Taiwan.

00:19:06.409 --> 00:19:14.010
But with the emergence of TSMC, and a growing
recognition of its centrality to the global

00:19:14.010 --> 00:19:20.000
economy and to the technologies of the future
including military technologies, it is increasingly

00:19:20.000 --> 00:19:25.940
part of the debate. So should the United States
defend Taiwan's democracy? Should it defend

00:19:25.940 --> 00:19:32.130
Taiwan because of its strategic location?
And also, should it defend Taiwan because

00:19:32.130 --> 00:19:38.260
it is the world's only producer of the leading
edge semiconductors? And how critical that

00:19:38.260 --> 00:19:41.720
is for US defense applications, for instance.

00:19:41.720 --> 00:19:48.570
SIERRA: I recently heard someone call TSMC
Taiwan's silicon shield . So how important

00:19:48.570 --> 00:19:50.920
is the company for Taiwan and its goals?

00:19:50.920 --> 00:19:58.870
SACKS: So I think that Taiwan and TSMC both
understand the centrality of TSMC to Taiwan.

00:19:58.870 --> 00:20:03.851
So for instance, Taiwan is having to ration
water right now for the island because it

00:20:03.851 --> 00:20:09.680
didn't have the same storms that it usually
gets. And what it's been doing is, it s let

00:20:09.680 --> 00:20:14.880
all the water flow to TSMC that it needs to
produce semiconductors, and it's rationing

00:20:14.880 --> 00:20:21.030
it for households elsewhere. And to me, that
really reveals that Taiwan understands the

00:20:21.030 --> 00:20:26.720
importance of TSMC to its economy, to its
society and also strategically.

00:20:26.720 --> 00:20:32.260
SIERRA: So, Taiwan understands that TSMC raises
its profile, and makes it a more essential

00:20:32.260 --> 00:20:37.450
partner across the world. The United States
understands that without TSMC, it would lose

00:20:37.450 --> 00:20:42.669
access to the chips that keep its economy
and military moving. And China, for its part,

00:20:42.669 --> 00:20:45.100
is navigating many of the same fears.

00:20:45.100 --> 00:20:49.710
SACKS: China's fears have already manifested
themselves, which is that the United States

00:20:49.710 --> 00:20:57.730
has been able to get TSMC to stop selling
chips to Chinese companies like Huawei. And

00:20:57.730 --> 00:21:02.049
while China likes to throw a lot of money
at problems like this, and assume that if

00:21:02.049 --> 00:21:09.299
it just spends billions and billions of dollars
it can catch up with TSMC, it has so far made

00:21:09.299 --> 00:21:14.070
very little progress. It's still years behind
TSMC. And while it's trying to catch up with

00:21:14.070 --> 00:21:18.580
TSMC, TSMC is already working on the next
level.

00:21:18.580 --> 00:21:25.001
SIERRA: Are we worried that semiconductors
might pave the road to conflict? I mean, how

00:21:25.001 --> 00:21:29.070
do we avoid the trap of thinking that conflict
is just inevitable?

00:21:29.070 --> 00:21:36.029
SACKS: Yeah. So to put this in perspective,
China's official line is that it still prefers

00:21:36.029 --> 00:21:43.390
peaceful unification. And so, what that means
is actually using an array of coercive tactics,

00:21:43.390 --> 00:21:50.860
short of force, to convince the Taiwanese
people that their future is inextricably tied

00:21:50.860 --> 00:21:57.120
to China. And that the best outcome for them
is unification with the mainland. And so,

00:21:57.120 --> 00:22:01.870
I think it's important to still keep in mind
that using force is their last option.

00:22:01.870 --> 00:22:08.159
SIERRA: Will China eventually attempt to reunify
Taiwan by force? If they did, would it pull

00:22:08.159 --> 00:22:13.710
the United States into war? And would the
U.S. and other countries lose access to TSMC

00:22:13.710 --> 00:22:19.750
s chips if China succeeded? These aren t questions
you commonly hear in the tech world, but they

00:22:19.750 --> 00:22:23.880
are the questions that many countries are
asking themselves as shortages spread and

00:22:23.880 --> 00:22:29.059
tensions increase. As a result, many have
decided that microchips should be considered

00:22:29.059 --> 00:22:33.600
strategically essential, and that they should
spend billions to figure out how to make them

00:22:33.600 --> 00:22:34.600
on their own.

00:22:34.600 --> 00:22:40.429
Arirang News: Prompted by the crippling Semiconductor
supply crunch around the world, U.S. President

00:22:40.429 --> 00:22:42.070
Joe Biden held a meeting with business leaders,
stressing the need to invest in what he called

00:22:42.070 --> 00:22:43.070
semiconductor infrastructure.

00:22:43.070 --> 00:22:46.040
Joe BIDEN: I have received a letter from 23
senators, bipartisan letter, and 42 house

00:22:46.040 --> 00:22:51.720
members republican and democrat. Supporting
the chips for America program. I ve been saying

00:22:51.720 --> 00:23:02.039
for some time now, China and the rest of the
world is not waiting, and there is no reason

00:23:02.039 --> 00:23:05.000
why Americans should wait.

00:23:05.000 --> 00:23:14.440
CNBC: So Beijing has made chips a top priority
for its 5 year plan. Independence is one of

00:23:14.440 --> 00:23:17.840
5 fundamentals" of China s economic development.

00:23:17.840 --> 00:23:23.429
SIERRA: But as it turns out chip independence
can t just be built overnight.

00:23:23.429 --> 00:23:32.760
SACKS: So president Biden has $50 billion
for the semiconductor industry in his infrastructure

00:23:32.760 --> 00:23:39.429
package, but that's just a drop in the bucket.
The semiconductor industry association estimates

00:23:39.429 --> 00:23:45.529
that the United States would have to spend
north of $1 trillion over a decade to become

00:23:45.529 --> 00:23:50.130
self-sufficient for its chips. It's going
to be very hard to become self-sufficient

00:23:50.130 --> 00:23:55.770
because it's frankly just cheaper to build
these in Taiwan and Taiwan has the engineering

00:23:55.770 --> 00:24:00.250
talent already in place. So it's going to
be very difficult for the United States to

00:24:00.250 --> 00:24:01.570
close this gap.

00:24:01.570 --> 00:24:05.600
CLARK: In the semiconductor business, they
talk about the learning curve, which is all

00:24:05.600 --> 00:24:11.370
about making more working chips on each wafer,
and getting their yield up. So you're more

00:24:11.370 --> 00:24:15.870
profitable and more productive. And so you
just have to keep producing these things,

00:24:15.870 --> 00:24:20.300
to learn all the little tweaks and knobs and
temperature settings and chemical adjustments

00:24:20.300 --> 00:24:26.610
you gotta make. So there is a tendency, once
a company gets in front, for them to stay

00:24:26.610 --> 00:24:27.860
in front.

00:24:27.860 --> 00:24:32.880
SIERRA: That doesn t mean the U.S. is giving
up the chase. If the Senate-approved bill

00:24:32.880 --> 00:24:39.330
passes in its current form, it would provide
$52 billion over five years to boost chip

00:24:39.330 --> 00:24:45.659
production and research. Intel plans to spend
$20 billion to build two new plants. And the

00:24:45.659 --> 00:24:51.919
U.S. is subsidizing TSMC s new $12 billion
factory in Arizona. Other world leaders are

00:24:51.919 --> 00:24:57.760
making big moves too - the EU plans to invest
up to $60 billion in chip design and production

00:24:57.760 --> 00:25:04.990
over the next 2-3 years. China plans to invest
$118 billion as part of its most recent five

00:25:04.990 --> 00:25:11.580
year plan. Chip production is expensive. But
money isn t the only factor.

00:25:11.580 --> 00:25:18.080
SIERRA: Is part of this competition a race
to get the best and brightest minds in the

00:25:18.080 --> 00:25:19.080
industry?

00:25:19.080 --> 00:25:23.779
CLARK: It is. It is very much so. And, you
know, all these companies hire, you know,

00:25:23.779 --> 00:25:29.860
all the PhDs they can and all the best electrical
engineers and computer scientists, you know,

00:25:29.860 --> 00:25:34.049
the US educational institutions produce the
best. Not the not the greatest number, necessarily,

00:25:34.049 --> 00:25:40.419
but the best and, but right now, I think that
the stats are like 60% or so of the students

00:25:40.419 --> 00:25:47.020
pursuing PhDs in those disciplines are foreign
born. So a lot of them tried to work here

00:25:47.020 --> 00:25:52.309
and use their educational degree to help get
a job here and stay in America, but the Trump

00:25:52.309 --> 00:25:58.430
administration made that very difficult. And
meanwhile, companies got stronger abroad.

00:25:58.430 --> 00:26:04.140
So some people say from Taiwan, for example,
who would have wanted to stay here, you know,

00:26:04.140 --> 00:26:09.710
cycle back to Taiwan, same thing was true
in mainland China, I think. So it is a race

00:26:09.710 --> 00:26:14.500
and consequently the semiconductor industry
association and people like that have lobbied

00:26:14.500 --> 00:26:19.700
hard for immigration policies that make it
easier for people to move from country to

00:26:19.700 --> 00:26:22.090
country and for educated people to stay here.

00:26:22.090 --> 00:26:26.909
SIERRA: Yeah, I mean, because traditionally,
we think of the US as having an advantage

00:26:26.909 --> 00:26:31.470
in getting the world's brightest minds, but
it seems like we may be losing that advantage.

00:26:31.470 --> 00:26:35.960
CLARK: Yeah in my opinion, we're shooting
ourselves in the foot because I think we should,

00:26:35.960 --> 00:26:40.070
you know, staple a green card to every PhD
in electrical engineering, you know.

00:26:40.070 --> 00:26:45.429
SACKS: In a sense it shows that the United
States doesn't have the talent that it once

00:26:45.429 --> 00:26:51.780
had. I mean, it's under-invested in R&amp;D and
it's undressed under-invested in STEM education.

00:26:51.780 --> 00:26:57.350
And interestingly, if you listen to TSMC's
founder, he will say that the United States

00:26:57.350 --> 00:27:03.140
doesn't have the engineering talent to make
it viable for TSMC to relocate these operations

00:27:03.140 --> 00:27:11.530
to the country. And he will point to Taiwan's
engineers as a big asset for TSMC going forward.

00:27:11.530 --> 00:27:16.620
MANOCHA: So you can invest all the money,
have a fab, but if you can't hire people,

00:27:16.620 --> 00:27:22.570
that's going to be a problem. I think that's
the way things are shaping up now. When countries

00:27:22.570 --> 00:27:29.020
start declaring the importance of semiconductors
for nationalistic reasons. There are two parts

00:27:29.020 --> 00:27:34.080
of this answer. The policy will be okay, whatever
I need to do to support the semiconductor

00:27:34.080 --> 00:27:38.940
industry in my country, policies will be in
that view. But the good thing is, the second

00:27:38.940 --> 00:27:44.770
part of this is that there's no one country
who can do everything from start to finish.

00:27:44.770 --> 00:27:49.570
Okay, so you have to depend on multiple countries.
So the policy makers will definitely start

00:27:49.570 --> 00:27:55.370
thinking, my gosh, this is a global supply
chain. We need to collaborate more with like-minded

00:27:55.370 --> 00:28:01.130
countries who are part of the integrated supply
chain, and we need to make sure not just only

00:28:01.130 --> 00:28:05.140
looking it from the nationalistic point of
view, but also from a global point of view

00:28:05.140 --> 00:28:11.090
that we got to work together with multiple
countries, because this supply chain is interdependent

00:28:11.090 --> 00:28:14.750
on many countries to make a chip.

00:28:14.750 --> 00:28:21.480
SIERRA: Chips have become an irreplaceable
part of our world. We need them for every

00:28:21.480 --> 00:28:27.000
call we make, every visit to the doctor, every
show we watch, every car we build. They re

00:28:27.000 --> 00:28:32.580
critical to economic growth, innovation, and
defense. The need to create them can lead

00:28:32.580 --> 00:28:39.080
to good things - investment in STEM education,
alliances, fruitful competition between researchers,

00:28:39.080 --> 00:28:44.240
and technological breakthroughs that better
our world. But like any other essential resource,

00:28:44.240 --> 00:28:50.690
they also have the power to fuel divisiveness,
trade disputes, nationalism and even armed

00:28:50.690 --> 00:28:55.720
conflict. The world is learning just how much
it depends on them. And that it can no longer

00:28:55.720 --> 00:29:01.730
tolerate the fragility revealed during the
pandemic. We need a better system. But what

00:29:01.730 --> 00:29:21.480
type of system will we build? For resources
used in this episode and more information,

